# Shell scripts must always check out with LF endings. Without this, a clone on
# Windows with core.autocrlf=true rewrites them to CRLF, and the CRLF shebang
# then fails at runtime with "env: bash\r: no such file or directory" (the \r is
# read as part of the interpreter name). Pinning eol=lf makes the checkout LF
# regardless of the user's autocrlf setting.
*.sh text eol=lf
